* ============================================================================
* 10_crosswalk_cascade_items_v1.dts
* PDLC crosswalk proof -- SQLite -> CSV -> x64base DBF -> CSV, one dataset.
*
* Status: CANDIDATE / FIRST RUN NOT YET RECORDED
* Owner: member.derald   authored_by: member.ai.claude.cowork
* Lane: PDLC historical migration demonstration
*
* WHY THIS EXISTS
*   The PDLC maturity review names the next decisive empirical milestone as
*   "one reproducible dataset crosswalk with row counts, field mappings, and
*   output checks". This script is that crosswalk for the portion the engine
*   can prove today. It is deliberately ONE table, traced completely, rather
*   than many tables traced partially.
*
* SUBJECT: Cascade Precision Mfg ERP, table Items
*   Chosen because it exercises what a crosswalk must actually prove:
*     - 18 rows x 13 columns (small enough to verify by eye)
*     - INTEGER / TEXT / REAL all present
*     - List_Price is NULL for several rows  -> NULL vs empty-string fidelity
*     - Active is an INTEGER boolean         -> logical/numeric mapping
*     - Item_ID is the primary key           -> identity preservation
*   Cascade is the right substrate: its manifest claims (34 tables / 9 views /
*   330 rows) were independently verified against the SQLite file and its
*   checksums verify, so discrepancies found here belong to the CONVERSION,
*   not to the source data.
*
* EVIDENCE CONTRACT
*   Every stage echoes a marker line beginning "XWALK" so a transcript can be
*   parsed deterministically. Capture the run with:
*     ./datarun.ps1 -CommandLines '...' > transcript.txt
*   or run this file with --script and redirect output. The transcript IS the
*   proof; an unrecorded successful run proves nothing (see the COBOL hop).
*
* SCOPE AND HONEST LIMITS
*   PROVES:     SQLite -> CSV -> x64base DBF -> CSV round trip with counts and
*               field maps captured at each hop.
*   DOES NOT PROVE: the fixed-record COBOL hop. That requires GnuCOBOL and a
*               preserved transcript; stage S0 below is a placeholder that
*               deliberately does nothing so the gap stays visible rather than
*               being quietly implied as complete.
*   DOES NOT PROVE: JSON. The JSON surface is design-stage; a JSON hop should
*               be added only once a canonical JSON emitter exists.
*
* PATHS
*   Run cwd is the runtime data root (dottalkpp\data), per datarun.ps1.
*   Scratch output goes to _drops/ following 05_roundtrip_dbf_csv_dbf.dts.
* ============================================================================

SET TALK ON
ECHO XWALK BEGIN cascade_items_v1

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* S0  fixed-record COBOL origin -- NOT PROVEN, INTENTIONALLY INERT
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* CORRECTED 2026-07-26. This stage originally read "SKIPPED -- no preserved
* GnuCOBOL transcript" and implied the hop was unbuilt. That was inherited from
* the PDLC review rather than verified, and it was wrong about the capability.
* src/edu/edu_cobol.cpp is status: supported and implements EXPORT STUDENTS /
* BUILD / RUN / TEST driving cobc -x -free. The artifacts exist. What was
* missing was only a transcript.
* The hop now has its own runnable proof: 12_cobol_fixed_record_v1.dts.
ECHO XWALK S0 COBOL DEFERRED see-12_cobol_fixed_record_v1.dts

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* S1  origin: the SEALED package -- no external database consulted
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Authority is the sealed Cascade package, not a live query. manifest.json
* carries per-table row counts and checksums.sha256 covers the CSVs, schema and
* seed data. Both were verified on 2026-07-26: 34 tables / 9 views / 330 rows,
* all 34 per-table counts matching, checksums OK.
*
* An earlier draft opened the SQLite file as a "type oracle". That was wrong on
* two counts: it made an external, writable database the authority for a proof
* about x64base, and it was unnecessary -- x64base derives types itself. The
* SQLite hop is removed. Nothing here reads it.
ECHO XWALK S1 ORIGIN sealed-package Items rows=18 cols=13
ECHO XWALK S1 END

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* S2  interchange: the CSV shipped with the package
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Items.csv is part of the sealed Cascade package and is covered by
* checksums.sha256, so it is treated as a fixed interchange artifact rather
* than something this run regenerates.
ECHO XWALK S2 INTERCHANGE csv cascade_precision_erp/data/Items.csv

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* S3  target: build the x64base table from the CSV
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ECHO XWALK S3 LOAD autodbf CROSSWALK_CASCADE_ERP_ITEMS_V1
AUTODBF X64 CROSSWALK_CASCADE_ERP_ITEMS_V1 FROM cascade_precision_erp/data/Items.csv HEADER INFER OVERWRITE
ECHO XWALK S3 END

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* S4  x64base introspection -- row count and field map on the target side
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ECHO XWALK S4 VERIFY x64base CROSSWALK_CASCADE_ERP_ITEMS_V1
USE CROSSWALK_CASCADE_ERP_ITEMS_V1
* COUNT prints the row count on its own line; ECHO does NOT expand %LASTCOUNT%
* (verified first run 2026-07-26 -- the marker printed literally). Read the
* count from the COUNT output line immediately following this marker.
ECHO XWALK S4 ROWS follows-on-next-line
COUNT
STRUCT
FIELDS
ECHO XWALK S4 END

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* S4b LONG-NAME SURVIVAL -- the schema as documentation
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Classic xBase caps field names at 10 characters, which is why legacy schemas
* are full of STDCOST and RORDRPT. x64base runs 128/128 by default (256/256
* max), so the migrated schema can carry its own meaning.
*
* Six of the 13 Items fields exceed the classic 10-char limit:
*   Description(11) Standard_Cost(13) List_Price(10 -- at the boundary)
*   Reorder_Point(13) Reorder_Qty(11) Cost_Method(11) Item_Type(9, safe)
* and the table name CROSSWALK_CASCADE_ERP_ITEMS_V1 is 30 characters.
*
* If any of these come back truncated, the migration has silently degraded the
* schema to a 1980s constraint and the field names stop being documentation.
* That is a migration FAILURE even when every row and value is correct.
ECHO XWALK S4B LONGNAMES expect-untruncated
FIELDS
ECHO XWALK S4B END

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* S5  data dictionary evidence -- the native field-mapping surface
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* DDICT is read-only. If the catalog has no rows for a freshly created table it
* is expected to report an honest physical-artifact status rather than a bare
* not-found, per its own contract notes.
ECHO XWALK S5 DDICT
DDICT STATUS
DDICT TABLES
DDICT FIELDS CROSSWALK_CASCADE_ERP_ITEMS_V1
ECHO XWALK S5 END

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* S6  return trip: x64base -> CSV
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ECHO XWALK S6 EXPORT
* Two corrections learned from runs 1 and 2, both recorded at the foot of this file:
*   run 1: "EXPORT CSV <file> HEADER ON DATES ISO" (copied from 05_roundtrip)
*          was REJECTED -- EXPORT grammar is EXPORT [TO] <file> [CSV|PIPE].
*   run 2: correct grammar, but "_drops/..." failed with "Unable to open ...
*          for write" -- dottalkpp\data\_drops does not exist. Writing to tmp/,
*          which the engine DECLARES at startup (TMP : dottalkpp\data\tmp).
EXPORT TO tmp/crosswalk_cascade_erp_items_v1_roundtrip.csv CSV
ECHO XWALK S6 END

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* S7  spot checks -- values that must survive the crossing
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Item_ID 1 is RAW-SHAFT-4140 with Standard_Cost 48.0 and a NULL List_Price.
* If NULL collapsed to 0 rather than empty, this is where it shows.
ECHO XWALK S7 SPOTCHECK
USE CROSSWALK_CASCADE_ERP_ITEMS_V1
LOCATE FOR SKU = "RAW-SHAFT-4140"
DISPLAY
ECHO XWALK S7 END

ECHO XWALK END cascade_items_v1

* ============================================================================
* EXPECTED EVIDENCE -- DERIVED FROM THE ENGINE'S OWN DOCUMENTED LADDER
*
*   THESIS: documentation proves the code. The expected structure below is not
*   borrowed from another database -- it is what x64base's OWN documented type
*   ladder says it must produce, replayed over Items.csv:
*
*     src/import/import_profile.cpp
*       classify_profile()      EMPTY / INTEGER / DECIMAL / DATE / LOGICAL /
*                               TEXT / LONGTEXT / MIXED
*       proposed_target_type()  INTEGER->N  DECIMAL->N  DATE->D  LOGICAL->L
*                               LONGTEXT->M  TEXT->C  MIXED->C  EMPTY->C
*
*   Two behaviours this derivation surfaced, which a casual reading gets wrong:
*
*   1. EMPTY CELLS DO NOT AFFECT CLASSIFICATION. update_profile() returns
*      immediately on an empty value, so it never sets a category flag and never
*      sets sawAnyNonEmpty. List_Price has 14 empty cells out of 18 and still
*      classifies DECIMAL -> N. Empties do not force MIXED.
*
*   2. "1"/"0" CLASSIFY AS INTEGER, NOT LOGICAL. is_logical_text() accepts
*      "1" and "0", but update_profile() tests is_integer_text() FIRST and
*      returns on match, so that branch is unreachable for numeric literals.
*      Active (1/0) must therefore land as N, not L. Only T/F/Y/N/TRUE/FALSE/
*      YES/NO reach LOGICAL. If Active ever imports as L, the ordering in
*      update_profile() changed and this test is the tripwire.
*
*   EXPECTED STRUCTURE after S3 (13 fields, in CSV order):
*     Item_ID        N(2)     INTEGER
*     SKU            C(14)    TEXT
*     Description    C(28)    TEXT
*     UOM            C(2)     TEXT
*     Item_Type      C(8)     TEXT
*     Cost_Method    C(8)     TEXT
*     Standard_Cost  N(5)     DECIMAL
*     List_Price     N(5)     DECIMAL   <- 14 empty cells, still DECIMAL
*     Reorder_Point  N(5)     DECIMAL
*     Reorder_Qty    N(5)     DECIMAL
*     Lead_Days      N(2)     INTEGER
*     Category       C(15)    TEXT
*     Active         N(1)     INTEGER   <- NOT L; see note 2
*   (Widths are the observed max content length; AUTODBF may pad. Compare the
*   TYPE LETTER strictly and treat width as advisory on first run.)
*
*   S4  ROWS        = 18
*   S4  field count = 13
*   S4B every field name returned at FULL length, none truncated to 10 chars;
*       table name CROSSWALK_CASCADE_ERP_ITEMS_V1 (30 chars) intact
*   S7  RAW-SHAFT-4140  Standard_Cost 48.0, List_Price EMPTY (not 0)
*
* PASS means: 18 rows, 13 fields, every type letter matching the table above,
* and List_Price still empty where the CSV was empty.
*
* A row-count match alone is NOT a pass. Type collapse and empty-to-zero are the
* failures this exists to catch, and both survive a count check.
*
* NOTE ON A CORRECTED DRAFT: an earlier version of this block carried two
* invented figures (a NULL count and a cost sum) that were never computed.
* They would have made a correct conversion appear to fail. Expected values in
* a proof harness must be derived from the code or the sealed data -- never
* written because they look plausible.
*
* ============================================================================
* FIRST RUN -- 2026-07-26, build Jul 26 2026 12:52:47, v0.6 (dfa8c136 dirty)
* ============================================================================
*
* RESULT: PASS on every data assertion. Two script defects, one repo finding.
*
* CONFIRMED BY RUN
*   AUTODBF OK, X64, "Imported rows: 18"        -> row fidelity
*   COUNT = 18, field count = 13                -> shape fidelity
*   All 13 TYPE LETTERS matched the derivation exactly:
*     N C C C C C N N N N N C N
*   LIST_PRICE = N despite 14 empty cells       -> prediction 1 CONFIRMED
*                                                  (empties never set a flag)
*   ACTIVE     = N, NOT L                       -> prediction 2 CONFIRMED
*                                                  (is_integer_text tested first)
*   S7 LIST_PRICE displayed EMPTY, not 0        -> NULL fidelity preserved
*   S7 STANDARD_COST = 48.0, SKU/CATEGORY intact
*
* LONG NAMES (S4B): PASS, and richer than expected. x64base keeps the FULL
*   logical name AND a legacy 10-char descriptor side by side:
*     STANDARD_COST  N(6,2)  descriptor=STANDARD_C
*     REORDER_POINT  N(5,1)  descriptor=REORDER_PO
*     DESCRIPTION    C(30)   descriptor=DESCRIPTIO
*   Table name CROSSWALK_CASCADE_ERP_ITEMS_V1 (30 chars) survived intact.
*   So the schema carries its own documentation while remaining addressable
*   from a classic 10-char world. Names ARE uppercased and normalized
*   (Item_ID -> ITEM_ID); case is not preserved.
*
* WIDTHS: allocated wider than observed content (SKU C(16) vs max 14,
*   DESCRIPTION C(30) vs 28, STANDARD_COST N(6,2) vs 5). Padding is expected;
*   the type letter is the strict assertion, width remains advisory.
*
* SCRIPT DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED
*   1. ECHO does NOT expand %LASTCOUNT% -- the marker printed literally as
*      "; XWALK S4 ROWS %LASTCOUNT%". Count is now read from the COUNT line.
*   2. EXPORT syntax was wrong (see S6). Corrected to EXPORT TO <file> CSV.
*
* REPO FINDING 1 -- tests/conversion/05_roundtrip_dbf_csv_dbf.dts IS NOT RUNNABLE
*   That file is cited as evidence that the DBF->CSV->DBF round trip is
*   "empirically demonstrated". Checked against the current command grammar,
*   four of its five lines cannot execute:
*     EXPORT CSV <f> HEADER ON DATES ISO   -- EXPORT takes [TO] <file> [CSV|PIPE]
*     IMPORT CSV <f> SCHEMA <j> REJECTS <r> -- IMPORT takes only <csvfile>
*     USE rt_students                       -- IMPORT appends into the OPEN table;
*                                              it never creates rt_students
*     %LASTCOUNT%                           -- does not expand (proven above)
*
* REPO FINDING 2 -- THE WHOLE tests/conversion SUITE TARGETS A MISSING DIRECTORY
*   Run 2 failed with "Unable to open _drops/... for write". dottalkpp\data\_drops
*   DOES NOT EXIST, and all five conversion tests write there:
*     01_dbf_to_csv_students.dts        02_csv_to_dbf_students.dts
*     03_dbf_to_sqlite_students.dts     04_sqlite_select_to_dbf_people.dts
*     05_roundtrip_dbf_csv_dbf.dts
*   So the failure is not confined to 05's grammar -- every conversion test would
*   fail at first write. The engine declares TMP (dottalkpp\data\tmp) at startup;
*   that is the correct scratch target, and this script now uses it.
*
*   Together these mean the PDLC review line "DBF/xBase and CSV conversion:
*   empirically demonstrated" rests on scripts that cannot currently run. The
*   capability is real -- this run proves the CSV -> x64base direction end to end
*   -- but the cited evidence is not executable. Same capability-vs-preserved-proof
*   gap the review correctly flags for COBOL. Either repair the suite (create
*   _drops or retarget to tmp/, fix 05's grammar) or downgrade the claim.
*
* STILL NOT PROVEN: the fixed-record COBOL hop (S0), and JSON.
* ============================================================================
